            0.059
          ],
          "content": "Exploring Mixtures and their Separation"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.856,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "N 79"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.088,
            0.269,
            0.106
          ],
          "content": "You may also try:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.774,
            0.143
          ],
          "content": "1. Place 1 – 2 mL of the saturated copper sulfate solution on a small glass plate or a lamination sheet."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.145,
            0.552,
            0.163
          ],
          "content": "2. Leave it for some time. What do you observe?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.166,
            0.762,
            0.185
          ],
          "content": "Did you get crystals? If yes, is this a good way to experiment? Explain."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.187,
            0.775,
            0.257
          ],
          "content": "Crystallization is a common technique for separating pure substances (crystals) from homogeneous mixtures. When new compounds are prepared, they are often accompanied by unwanted impurities. Crystallization helps to separate the desired pure substance from the undesired impurities."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.261,
            0.162,
            0.31
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.265,
            0.373,
            0.283
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think as a Scientist"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.294,
            0.907,
            0.343
          ],
          "content": "If a hot, saturated solution of copper sulfate is cooled rapidly in ice-cold water, smaller and less well-formed crystals will form than if it is cooled slowly at room temperature. How would you design and perform an experiment to test this **hypothesis**?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.343,
            0.862,
            0.361
          ],
          "content": "**Hint:** Prepare a hot saturated solution of copper sulfate and divide it into two equal parts."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.385,
            0.19,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.198,
            0.389,
            0.388,
            0.409
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Pause and Ponder"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.418,
            0.901,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": "4. Refer to the solubility curves given in Activity 5.2. If equal masses of hot, saturated solutions of compounds ‘A’ and ‘B’ are cooled from 80 °C to 60 °C, which solution is likely to deposit more solid?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.464,
            0.901,
            0.497
          ],
          "content": "5. Will there be any change in the size of common salt crystals if the rate of evaporation is increased or decreased? Explain."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.523,
            0.498,
            0.542
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 5.4: Let us describe a process"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.544,
            0.774,
            0.58
          ],
          "content": "Observe Fig. 5.9, it shows how salt crystals are obtained from seawater. Can you describe the process in your own words?"
